    <h2>The three numbers, and where each one lives<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Cinemas contains exactly three numbers that matter, and they lead one into the next: the price boards feed the terminal, the cabinet in the hallway hands you the extinguisher, the extinguisher creates the code, and the code opens the door.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>8, 11, 13</b> — the price tags on MENU 1, MENU 2 and MENU 3, on the lit boards above the lobby counters. They are small, but they are not hidden: they are the only prices in the room.</li>
      <li><b>32</b> — what 8 + 11 + 13 comes to. The terminal under MENU 1 has a calculator keypad with a plus and an equals, so type the sum in the form the footage shows, <b>8+11+13</b>, and press <b>=</b>.</li>
      <li><b>3581</b> — burned into the BURNING CITY poster after you extinguish it, read left to right, typed into the EXIT door keypad.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Everything else that looks like a code is not one. The three counters are numbered 1, 2 and 3 and that is just labelling. The glowing numbers over the screen doors are screen numbers. The arrows on the cinema screen are a pattern to copy, not digits. If you find yourself trying to read a number off a popcorn case, you have already solved the level twice over.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The poster is on fire when you arrive.</b> Most players see "BURNING CITY" and read it as artwork. It is a real fire, it is in the way of the code, and the thing that puts it out is in another room. Until you have dealt with it, the level has no code at all.</li>
      <li><b>The cable.</b> It is white on a white-ish crisp bag and it is behind the counter, not in the open. It is also the only item in the level whose use our footage never shows, so if you are stuck, check the inventory for Cable before you go looking for another puzzle.</li>
      <li><b>The key and the selfie stick.</b> The rack responds to the stick and to nothing else. Tapping the key directly does nothing, which reads as "not interactive" rather than "out of reach".</li>
      <li><b>The price boards.</b> Three separate boards, three separate prices, one terminal. Players tend to type the price of the board that is on the screen (8) and stop. The terminal wants all three.</li>
      <li><b>The seat chart.</b> It looks like the most solvable puzzle in the level and it is the least documented. It is a marking screen, not a code: the row and seat you need are printed on the ticket from the desk, not on the chart.</li>
      <li><b>The extinguisher cabinet.</b> In the hallway, on the wall among the red doors and posters, with a glass door that has to be opened first. It is easy to walk past because the hallway's real draw is the two numbered screen doors.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warn">
      <b>What we could not verify.</b> Three things, so you know what is fact and what is not. (1) <b>The marked seats on the chart.</b> The ticket is legible — THEATER 01, SEAT E11 — but the completed chart is not: at playback resolution the red cells are a few pixels wide, our two independent reads produced different seat lists, and the recording marks more than one seat. We give you E11 from the ticket and stop there. (2) <b>The five-bar pattern.</b> The footage shows the arrows on the cinema screen and it shows the panel being worked, but never a held, readable target state, so there is no bar-by-bar answer on this page. (3) <b>The terminal's exact expected input.</b> Our recording types the expression 8+11+13 and presses equals, and the screen clears; the footage never shows it rejecting a wrong entry, so we cannot tell you whether typing 32 also works. If the terminal refuses your entry, type the expression exactly as above.
    </div>

    <h2>Where this sits in Chapter 2<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Cinemas is level 2 of the 26 in Chapter 2, "No Honor Among Thieves" — the chapter that swaps the single-room format of Chapter 1 for whole buildings. Level 1 is the <a href="/rooms-and-exits/chapter-2-level-1/">Cell Phone Store</a>; after Cinemas the chapter moves on to the Pharmacy, the Optical Store and the Gym. If you came here from a search and want the rest of the chapter in one place, the <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits hub</a> lists every level with its status and its walkthrough.</p>

    <h2>FAQ<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <details><summary>What is the exit door code in Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 2 (Cinemas)?</summary><p><b>3581</b>. The four digits appear on the BURNING CITY poster in the lobby, but only after you have put the fire on the poster out with the fire extinguisher from the red wall cabinet in the hallway. They are printed in a horizontal row on the charred poster. Type 3581 into the keypad on the red exit door and press the tick.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What do the three menu boards in the cinema lobby mean?</summary><p>They are the numbers for the terminal puzzle. The boards above the counters read <b>MENU 1</b> with a price of 8, <b>MENU 2</b> with 11 and <b>MENU 3</b> with 13. Our recording opens the terminal under the MENU 1 board, types <b>8+11+13</b> on the calculator keypad and presses the equals key, and the screen clears back to its blue idle state.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where is the fire extinguisher in Cinemas?</summary><p>In the hallway, in a red wall cabinet with a glass front. The cabinet door swings open to the left. Take the extinguisher from the cabinet, go back to the BURNING CITY poster in the lobby, select the extinguisher and use it on the poster. The flames go out and the code 3581 is left on the burnt poster.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How do you get the key off the wooden rack?</summary><p>With the selfie stick. The rack is a wooden bar with three pegs mounted high on the lobby wall and the key hangs on the middle peg, out of reach. Select the selfie stick from the inventory, extend it at the rack, and the key comes off into your inventory.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where is the cable in Chapter 2 Level 2?</summary><p>In the crisp bags on the snack shelf behind counter 1, next to the EXPRESS CAFE sign. Zoom in on the bags, tap the white coiled cable lying across them, and the pickup screen labels it <b>Cable</b>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does the seat map need a specific set of seats?</summary><p>The ticket the printer produces names <b>THEATER 01 / SEAT E11</b>, so mark row E, seat 11. The chart is six rows, A to F from the bottom up, with seats 1 to 12 across, and tapping a seat fills it red. We do not publish the full set of marked seats because the red cells are only a few pixels wide at playback resolution and our reads of the completed chart disagreed — but E11 is unambiguous on the ticket and was read from three separate frames.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is the reward for finishing Cinemas?</summary><p>50 coins. The level closes on the LEVEL COMPLETED screen with <b>+50</b> in the top reward panel and a level time of 8:09, plus the piggy bank panel underneath.</p></details>

